    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250",
              "source": "UniProtKB",
              "id": "Q03513"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "24907906"
            }
          ],
          "value": "The N-terminus (residues 1-209) has carbonic anhydrase (CA) activity (PubMed:24907906). The C-terminus has 3 repeats that are similar to the small subunit of RuBisCO (rbcS), called SSUL. The SSUL are connected by flexible linkers. The N-terminal domain probably forms a scaffold on which CcmN and maybe other carboxysomal proteins assemble, while the C-terminus binds and coalesces RuBisCO (By similarity)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "DOMAIN"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "17675289"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "24907906"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "24907906"
            }
          ],
          "value": "The first amino acid of the short form (equivalent to Val-226) is not seen in Edman degradation, while Ser-230 may be post-translationally modified (PubMed:17675289). Migrates in gels as 2 about equal forms of about 60 and 35 kDa (called M58 and M35) (PubMed:24907906). They are probably the result of alternative translation initiation (Probable)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "PTM"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Belongs to the gamma-class carbonic anhydrase family"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "SIMILARITY"
    }
